# Array Along Path

Generated `array-along-path` surface. Medium risk; sandbox until live placement
and Undo verification. Ribbon: **{{ tab_display }} / Elements / Array Along Path**.

1. Open an editable project. Pick connected model or detail curves, then a
   point-located family instance or group. Escape cancels without changing the model.
   A branded guide above the active canvas shows the current selection step,
   what to pick and Finish/Esc guidance; it closes before the preview opens.
2. Adjust spacing or count, tangent alignment, side offset, original placement offset and
   seeded position/rotation jitter. Pan, zoom, fit, inspect or pin copies.
   **By count / Copies** includes the path start when offset or scatter makes
   it a new location. Otherwise that position stays reserved for the original.
   The requested number of new copies covers the path, including the end when
   there is more than one position. The slider defaults to 1–50; Slider max changes its range up to 2000 and
   remembers that choice. Typing a larger count expands the slider range.
   Reducing Slider max also reduces a count above that maximum.
   Any location overlapping the original is skipped and reported in the footer.
3. Place copies submits the preview and closes the web window. Python validates
   the plan and source again, then copies and rotates elements in the original
   pyRevit command context, in one transaction. Source-anchor duplicates are
   excluded from both the placement count and execution. Undo restores the model.
   The result lists path length, distribution, offsets, orientation, variation
   when used, copies placed and skipped source overlaps in readable terms.

The preview is a tessellated **plan projection**, not a full 3D model. Feet are
Revit internal units; non-horizontal paths should be reviewed in Revit. Shared
nested families and groups contribute source outlines; meshes use a convex hull;
a bounding box is the last-resort fallback and is disclosed in the source note.
Disconnected curves are skipped with a visible warning. Maximum 2000 stations.
The original's outline and **Original** marker stay at its actual anchor location,
independent of offsets, alignment and scatter; it is excluded from new-copy totals.

**Side offset** is measured sideways from the path at each copy. **Include original
placement offset**, beside Side offset in Distribution, additionally carries the
original's displacement from its nearest path point into every copy as a fixed
shift in model coordinates. That shift does not turn with the curve. Unchecking
it removes only that original displacement; Side offset and position variation
still apply. Copies lie on the path when all those position offsets are zero.

The control pane has a draggable/keyboard separator (arrows, Home to reset),
per-user width persistence, and a stacked layout below 760px. Fit view and motion
replay are explicit actions. CSS and native Web Animations provide interruptible
feedback, a moving mode indicator, contextual seed feedback, and station reveal.
Both placement and orientation checkboxes draw/erase their checkmarks and respond with a brief
scale animation while retaining native checkbox keyboard and screen reader behavior.
Reduced motion removes travel, reveal and stagger by default; Preview motion
allows an explicit opt-in for this window. Geometry transitions stop
above 150 stations so large arrays remain responsive. No CDN or npm is needed.

Open `tool.html` in a browser for a labeled demo using synthetic geometry; Apply
only simulates there. The same local assets and fonts load inside WebView2.
Code is adapted from Rob's Array Along Path tool (source recorded in Formwork
release record R07). Source marks, commercial fonts and library bootstrap were
replaced with generated firm assets. Web host: `{{ package }}`.

Live gate: path picks/cancellation; family/group copies; source unchanged;
spacing/count/offset/jitter match the preview; pinned/hosted sources fail with
complete rollback; Undo; Revit transaction errors; close/Escape; light/dark icons.
